{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T14:34:01.452354", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "no_mem", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_ad37a656-079a-49f9-a493-7b739c9167d1", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: On July 15, 2008, Phys.org published an article about a catastrophe. Find the explosive force of this catastrophe according to Encyclopedia Britannica, then find the name of the US nuclear test that had the same yield. Your answer should only be the last word of the name of the test.", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "The Phys.org article published on July 15, 2008, covered the Tunguska event of 1908. According to Encyclopedia Britannica, the explosive force of the Tunguska catastrophe was approximately 10–15 megatons of TNT (commonly cited as about 15 megatons). The U.S. nuclear test with a yield of 15 megatons was \"Castle Bravo.\" The last word of that test's name is:\n\nBravo", "expected": "Bravo", "score": 1.0, "em": 1.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": null, "fb_mode": "gold", "code_rev": "3f7502f7.ff40ae22", "critic_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "judgment", "score_provenance": "gold", "judge_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "protocol_hash": "c6b1ff544be8", "prof_total_s": 105.41, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 105.41, "prof_tok_in": 599, "prof_tok_out": 6485, "prof_llm_calls": 2}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T14:34:12.043381", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "no_mem", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_00d579ea-0889-4fd9-a771-2c8d79835c8d", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: Assuming scientists in the famous youtube video The Thinking Machine (Artificial Intelligence in the 1960s) were interviewed the same year, what is the name of the scientist predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots? Answer using the format First name Last name", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "I need to find information about the YouTube video \"The Thinking Machine (Artificial Intelligence in the 1960s)\" and identify the scientist who predicted sooner thinking machines or robots. Let me search for this.</think:6124c78e>I'll search for information about this specific video and its content.</think:6124c78e>I need to find the specific video and its transcript or description to identify which scientist predicted sooner thinking machines or robots. Let me search.</think:6124c78e>I'll search for the video details and transcript.</think:6124c78e>I need to search for the video content. Since I cannot actually use tools in this environment (the system says \"No tools are available in this environment. Do not emit tool-call markup. Answer directly, reasoning in plain text\"), I must reason from my own knowledge and any accessible memory.\n\nWait, the system prompt says: \"No tools are available in this environment. Do not emit tool-call markup. Answer directly, reasoning in plain text, and end with the final answer.\"\n\nBut the user prompt says: \"You are an expert research assistant capable of multi-step reasoning and tool use. You have access to web search, file reading, code execution, and computation tools.\" and \"Strategy for answering questions: 1. ANALYZE... 2. SEARCH: Use web search...\"\n\nHowever, the top-level instruction says \"No tools are available in this environment. Do not emit tool-call markup. Answer directly, reasoning in plain text, and end with the final answer.\"\n\nSo I must answer directly without tool calls, using my reasoning and knowledge.\n\nLet me recall what I know about \"The Thinking Machine\" video. There is a well-known 1961 documentary/film called \"The Thinking Machine\" produced by CBS or Bell Labs? Actually, \"The Thinking Machine\" is a 1962 film (or 1961) featuring interviews with pioneers of AI. It is often uploaded to YouTube with the title \"The Thinking Machine (Artificial Intelligence in the 1960s)\".\n\nThe scientists featured include:\n- Marvin Minsky\n- John McCarthy\n- Oliver Selfridge\n- Claude Shannon (maybe?)\n- Herbert Simon?\n- Allen Newell?\n\nLet's recall the content. In the video, they discuss machines that can think. There is a part where they ask about when a machine will be able to beat a human at chess, or when a machine will pass the Turing test, or when we'll have robots.\n\nSpecifically, I recall a scene where a scientist predicts that within a certain number of years, machines will be able to do various things. Oliver Selfridge? Or maybe Marvin Minsky?\n\nActually, there is a famous clip where they ask \"When will a machine be able to beat a human at chess?\" and someone says \"within 10 years\" (which was overly optimistic). That was likely from a 1958 or 1960s interview. In the \"Thinking Machine\" video, there is a segment where they discuss predictions.\n\nLet me think about the exact video. \"The Thinking Machine\" (1961) directed by? It features:\n- Dr. Marvin Minsky (MIT)\n- Dr. John McCarthy (Stanford, then MIT?)\n- Dr. Oliver Selfridge (Lincoln Labs)\n- Dr. Claude Shannon (Bell Labs)\n- Dr. Nathaniel Rochester (IBM)\n- Maybe Herbert Simon and Allen Newell?\n\nIn the video, there is a discussion about a \"robot\" or \"thinking machine\". One of them says something like \"I think we will have a machine that can think within 20 years\" or something.\n\nWait, the question says: \"Assuming scientists in the famous youtube video The Thinking Machine (Artificial Intelligence in the 1960s) were interviewed the same year, what is the name of the scientist predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots?\"\n\nThis implies that in the video, multiple scientists give predictions about when thinking machines or robots will exist, and one predicts a sooner date than the others. The question asks for the name of the scientist predicting the *sooner* thinking machines or robots.\n\nLet me recall the video. I remember watching \"The Thinking Machine\" on YouTube. It's a black-and-white film. At one point, the interviewer asks about the future. Oliver Selfridge says something like \"I think that within 10 or 15 years we will have machines that can do most of the things that humans can do.\" Or maybe Marvin Minsky says \"within a generation\".\n\nActually, there is a specific quote: \"I would say that within ten years, a machine will be able to do any intellectual task that a man can do.\" Who said that? That sounds like Herbert Simon (1957: \"within ten years a digital computer will be the world's chess champion\" - actually Simon said in 1957 that within ten years a computer would beat a human at chess, and within ten years a computer would discover and prove an important mathematical theorem). But that was Herbert Simon.\n\nBut is Herbert Simon in \"The Thinking Machine\"? I'm not sure. The Thinking Machine (1961) features Minsky, McCarthy, Selfridge, Shannon, Rochester, and maybe others.\n\nLet me search my memory: The YouTube video \"The Thinking Machine (Artificial Intelligence in the 1960s)\" is from the channel \"The Computer History Museum\" or similar? Actually, it's often from \"AI: The Thinking Machine\".\n\nI recall a specific part: The film shows a panel. They ask: \"When will we have a machine that can think?\" One says \"25 years\", another says \"50 years\", another says \"100 years\", and one says \"sooner than you think\" or \"within 20 years\".\n\nWait, there is a well-known segment where they discuss \"The year 2000\" or something.\n\nLet me think about Oliver Selfridge. He was known for \"Pandemonium\" and was optimistic. Marvin Minsky was also optimistic.\n\nActually, I remember a specific line from the video: \"I think that we are on the threshold of making machines that can think, and I would guess that within twenty years we will have a machine that can do almost anything a human brain can do.\" That might be Minsky.\n\nBut the question says \"predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots\". Maybe there is a clear contrast: one scientist says \"within 20 years\" and another says \"within 100 years\" or \"never\". The one with the sooner prediction is the answer.\n\nLet me try to recall the exact scientists and their predictions in that film. The film \"The Thinking Machine\" (1961) includes:\n- Moderator: maybe Edward R. Murrow? No, that's \"The Computer and the Brain\"?\nActually, it's a Bell Telephone Laboratories film: \"The Thinking Machine\" (1961). It features:\n- Dr. Marvin Minsky\n- Dr. John McCarthy\n- Dr. Oliver Selfridge\n- Dr. Claude Shannon\n- Dr. Nathaniel Rochester\n\nIn the film, they discuss a program that proves theorems, plays checkers, etc. At the end, they are asked about the future. I think Oliver Selfridge says: \"I think that within ten years we will have a machine that can carry on a conversation as well as a human being.\" Or something like that.\n\nWait, I found a memory: In the video, they ask \"When will a machine be able to beat a human at chess?\" and someone says \"Not in my lifetime\" or \"within 50 years\". But actually, in the 1960s, many thought it would be soon.\n\nLet's think about the specific phrasing: \"predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots\". Could it be that one scientist predicts \"thinking machines\" sooner, and another predicts \"robots\" sooner? Or the scientist who predicts the sooner arrival of thinking machines/robots compared to others?\n\nMaybe the video has a part where they ask about \"robots\" and \"thinking machines\". One says \"robots are a long way off\" but \"thinking machines are near\". Or vice versa.\n\nActually, I recall a quote from John McCarthy: \"I think it will be possible to make a machine that can think within a few decades.\" Minsky: \"I think we will have a thinking machine in 20 years.\"\n\nBut let's be precise. The question says: \"Assuming scientists in the famous youtube video The Thinking Machine (Artificial Intelligence in the 1960s) were interviewed the same year, what is the name of the scientist predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots?\"\n\nThis might refer to a specific comparison in the video. For example, in the video, they might show a scientist saying \"I predict thinking machines in 20 years\" and another saying \"I predict robots in 50 years\". Or maybe two scientists give different years for the same thing, and we need the one with the sooner year.\n\nWait, could it be about the \"sooner\" as in \"sooner than others\"? The scientist predicting the sooner (i.e., earliest) timeline.\n\nLet me think about the film's content. I remember a scene where they are sitting around a table. The interviewer asks: \"Do you think that a machine will ever be able to think?\" They all say yes. Then: \"When?\" One says \"within 20 years\" (Minsky?), one says \"within 50 years\" (Shannon?), one says \"within 100 years\" (Rochester?), and one says \"within 10 years\" (Selfridge?). The one with the shortest time frame is the answer.\n\nI think Oliver Selfridge was the most optimistic. He said something like \"within 10 to 15 years\". But I'm not entirely sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's Marvin Minsky. In a 1960s interview, Minsky said \"In from three to eight years we will have a machine with the general intelligence of an average human being.\" Actually, that quote is often attributed to Minsky from 1967 or so: \"In from three to eight years we will have a machine with the general intelligence of an average human being.\" That is a very soon prediction! That would be the sooner prediction.\n\nWait, that quote: \"In from three to eight years we will have a machine with the general intelligence of an average human being.\" This is from Marvin Minsky in \"The Thinking Machine\" or a similar documentary? Let me recall. Minsky said this in a 1967 interview? Or was it in the 1961 film? Actually, the film is from the early 60s. If he said 3 to 8 years from 1961, that's 1964-1969. That is very soon.\n\nBut the question says \"assuming scientists ... were interviewed the same year\". This might mean the video was published/interviewed in a certain year, and we need to compute something? Or just \"what is the name of the scientist predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots?\" The \"assuming interviewed the same year\" might be a distractor or just clarifying that the prediction year is relative to the interview year.\n\nActually, maybe the video is \"The Thinking Machine\" and it features a specific segment where a scientist predicts \"thinking machines\" in a certain year and \"robots\" in a certain year, and another scientist predicts differently. The question: \"what is the name of the scientist predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots?\" Could be asking: among the scientists, who predicted the sooner arrival? i.e., the most optimistic.\n\nLet me search my memory of the exact video. The YouTube video \"The Thinking Machine (Artificial Intelligence in the 1960s)\" has a description: \"A 1961 documentary about artificial intelligence, featuring Marvin Minsky, John McCarthy, Oliver Selfridge, Claude Shannon, and Nathaniel Rochester.\"\n\nIn the video, at around 25:00, they discuss the future. The question posed: \"When do you think we'll have a machine that can think?\" Answers:\n- Oliver Selfridge: \"I think we'll have a thinking machine within 10 to 15 years.\" (or something)\n- Marvin Minsky: \"I think it will take about 20 years.\"\n- John McCarthy: \"I would say 30 to 40 years.\"\n- Claude Shannon: \"I'm more conservative, maybe 50 years or more.\"\n- Nathaniel Rochester: \"Maybe 100 years.\"\n\nThus the sooner prediction is from Oliver Selfridge. But I'm not sure if Selfridge said 10-15 years.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Minsky said the 3-8 years quote. Let me verify the quote: \"In from three to eight years we will have a machine with the general intelligence of an average human being.\" This is widely attributed to Marvin Minsky, from a 1967 interview with Life magazine or something? Actually, it's from a 1960s TV program. Wait, I think it's from the 1961 film? Let me think.\n\nActually, the quote \"In from three to eight years we will have a machine with the general intelligence of an average human being\" is from Marvin Minsky in \"The Thinking Machine\" (1961)? If so, that is extremely soon (by 1964-1969) and would be the sooner prediction compared to others who said decades.\n\nBut wait, there is also a famous quote from Herbert Simon in 1957: \"It is not my aim to surprise or shock you... but the most important discovery of this century is that machines can think.\" And he predicted chess champion in 10 years (by 1967). But Simon is not in the Thinking Machine film (I think).\n\nLet's consider the video title exactly: \"The Thinking Machine (Artificial Intelligence in the 1960s)\". If I search my memory of YouTube, there is a video uploaded by \"The Film Archives\" or \"AI\" with that title. It's a 1961 Bell Labs film. The scientists are Minsky, McCarthy, Selfridge, Shannon, Rochester.\n\nI recall a specific moment: The host asks \"Who among you thinks that a machine will be able to think soonest?\" Or they each give a number. Oliver Selfridge says \"I think we will have a machine that can think in about ten years.\" Marvin Minsky says \"I think it will be within twenty years.\" John McCarthy says \"I would say within fifty years.\" Claude Shannon says \"I think it's a long way off.\" Nathaniel Rochester says \"Maybe a hundred years.\"\n\nBut I'm not entirely confident. Let me think about another angle. The question says \"predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots\". The use of \"or robots\" might indicate that in the video, they distinguish between thinking machines and robots. Perhaps one scientist says \"thinking machines will come sooner\" and another says \"robots will come sooner\"? Or maybe the scientist predicts *both* thinking machines and robots, and his prediction is sooner than the others.\n\nWait, maybe the video has a part where they ask: \"When will we have robots?\" and someone says \"sooner than thinking machines\" or vice versa. Actually, I remember a line: \"I think we will have thinking machines sooner than we will have robots that can move around.\" Or something.\n\nLet's try to recall the exact transcript. I think there is a part where Oliver Selfridge says: \"I think that we will have a thinking machine sooner than we will have a robot.\" Or maybe Marvin Minsky says: \"I think robots are a long way off, but thinking machines are not.\"\n\nAlternatively, the question might be simpler: In the video, they ask about when machines will think. One scientist predicts a sooner date. Who is it? The answer is likely Oliver Selfridge or Marvin Minsky.\n\nLet me check the exact phrasing of the question: \"Assuming scientists in the famous youtube video The Thinking Machine (Artificial Intelligence in the 1960s) were interviewed the same year, what is the name of the scientist predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots? Answer using the format First name Last name\"\n\n\"interviewed the same year\" - this might refer to the fact that the video is a compilation or something? Or maybe the video is from a specific year, say 1961, and they were interviewed in 1961. The \"assuming... same year\" might be to clarify that we don't need to worry about video upload date vs interview date; just use the interview year (the 1960s) as given.\n\nBut why mention \"same year\"? Could it be that the video features scientists interviewed in different years? No, it's a single film from 1961. So they were interviewed the same year (1961). The question might be generated by an automated system that adds \"assuming interviewed the same year\" to handle date-relative predictions (like \"in 10 years\" -> 1971). But the question just asks for the name of the scientist, not the year. So the \"same year\" part is irrelevant to the final answer format, just context.\n\nThus, I need to name the scientist who predicted the sooner thinking machines or robots.\n\nLet me try to be as accurate as possible. I will recall the film \"The Thinking Machine\" (1961). The participants:\n- Marvin Minsky\n- John McCarthy\n- Oliver Selfridge\n- Claude Shannon\n- Nathaniel Rochester\n\nAt the end of the film, they discuss predictions. I found a memory: There is a book \"The Thinking Machine\" or references to this film. In the film, Oliver Selfridge says he thinks a machine will be able to think within 10 years. Marvin Minsky says 20 years. John McCarthy says 30 years. Claude Shannon says he doesn't know but it's a long way. Nathaniel Rochester says maybe never or 100 years.\n\nBut wait, I also recall a specific quote from the film: \"I think that within eight years we will have a machine that can do any intellectual task a man can do.\" That sounds like Minsky's \"three to eight years\" quote. If Minsky said 3-8 years, that's sooner than 10 years. So Minsky would be the answer.\n\nLet me verify the \"three to eight years\" quote. A quick mental search: Marvin Minsky, in a 1967 interview? Actually, the quote \"In from three to eight years we will have a machine with the general intelligence of an average human being\" is often cited as from Minsky in \"The Thinking Machine\" (1961) or from a 1960s TV appearance. Wait, I think it's from a 1967 interview with Minsky for the \"Tomorrow\" show or something. But the video \"The Thinking Machine (Artificial Intelligence in the 1960s)\" might include archival footage. However, the video itself is titled as being from the 1960s.\n\nLet's think about the YouTube video specifically. If I recall the comments or description: \"The Thinking Machine (Artificial Intelligence in the 1960s) - Featuring Marvin Minsky, John McCarthy, Oliver Selfridge, Claude Shannon, and Nathaniel Rochester. In this film, they discuss the early days of AI. At the end, they make predictions. Oliver Selfridge predicts thinking machines in 10 years, Minsky in 20, McCarthy in 30-40, Shannon says long time, Rochester says 100 years.\"\n\nActually, I think I remember watching it. The scientist who predicts the soonest is Oliver Selfridge. He says something like \"I think we'll have a machine that can think within ten years, and maybe even sooner.\" He is smiling and optimistic. Minsky says \"twenty years\". So Selfridge is sooner.\n\nBut wait, there is also a part where they talk about \"robots\". Selfridge might say \"robots are further off\". The question says \"predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots\". This could mean: who predicted that thinking machines would come sooner than robots? Or who predicted the sooner of the two (thinking machines or robots)? The phrasing \"predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots\" is ambiguous. It could mean \"the scientist who predicted (the sooner (thinking machines or robots))\" i.e., the scientist with the earliest prediction for either thinking machines or robots. Or \"the scientist predicting that thinking machines would come sooner than robots\".\n\nLet's parse: \"the name of the scientist predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots\". This could be read as \"the scientist predicting the sooner [arrival of] thinking machines or robots\" = the scientist who gave the soonest prediction for thinking machines or for robots. That would be the most optimistic overall.\n\nAlternatively: \"the scientist predicting the sooner [of] thinking machines or robots\" = the scientist who said thinking machines will come sooner than robots (or vice versa). But \"predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots\" is awkward.\n\nMaybe it means: In the video, they ask about thinking machines and robots. One scientist predicts thinking machines will happen sooner; another predicts robots will happen sooner. Who is the one predicting the sooner one? No, that doesn't make sense.\n\nLet's look at the exact wording: \"what is the name of the scientist predicting the sooner thinking machines or robots?\" I think it means: among the scientists, who predicted that thinking machines/robots would arrive sooner (i.e., gave the earliest timeline). So we need the most optimistic scientist.\n\nNow, which scientist in that video gave the earliest timeline? I need to be sure.\n\nLet me recall the film's ending. The film \"The Thinking Machine\" (1961) ends with each scientist giving a final thought. Oliver Selfridge says: \"I th", "expected": "Claude Shannon", "score": 1.0, "em": 1.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "3", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": null, "fb_mode": "gold", "code_rev": "3f7502f7.ff40ae22", "critic_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "judgment", "score_provenance": "gold", "judge_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "protocol_hash": "c6b1ff544be8", "prof_total_s": 116.0, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 116.0, "prof_tok_in": 579, "prof_tok_out": 6737, "prof_llm_calls": 2}
