{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T15:02:26.175571", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "no_mem", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_5cfb274c-0207-4aa7-9575-6ac0bd95d9b2", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: Each cell in the attached spreadsheet represents a plot of land. The color of the cell indicates who owns that plot. Green cells are plots owned by Earl Smith. Can Earl walk through every plot he owns (and no other plots) and return to his starting plot without backtracking? For this question, consider backtracking to be any instance where Earl would enter a plot of land he had already entered since leaving his starting plot.", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "The referenced spreadsheet containing the grid of plots and their color-coded ownership is not included in this conversation. Without the specific layout of the green cells (Earl Smith’s plots), it is impossible to determine whether those plots form a connected region that admits a closed path visiting each exactly once without re-entry (a Hamiltonian cycle). Therefore, the question cannot be answered from the information provided.\n\nCannot be determined (attachment missing)", "expected": "No", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "1", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 2, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": null, "fb_mode": "gold", "code_rev": "3f7502f7.ff40ae22", "critic_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "guarded", "score_provenance": "gold", "judge_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "protocol_hash": "cda8cfafe9da", "prof_total_s": 693.38, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 693.38, "prof_tok_in": 649, "prof_tok_out": 2586, "prof_llm_calls": 2}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T15:02:28.495395", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "no_mem", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_544b7f0c-173a-4377-8d56-57b36eb26ddf", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: In Valentina Re’s contribution to the 2017 book “World Building: Transmedia, Fans, Industries”, what horror movie does the author cite as having popularized metalepsis between a dream world and reality? Use the complete name with article if any.", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "The horror movie cited by Valentina Re in her contribution to the 2017 book *World Building: Transmedia, Fans, Industries* as having popularized metalepsis between a dream world and reality is *A Nightmare on Elm Street*.\n\nA Nightmare on Elm Street", "expected": "A Nightmare on Elm Street", "score": 1.0, "em": 1.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 2, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": null, "fb_mode": "gold", "code_rev": "3f7502f7.ff40ae22", "critic_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "guarded", "score_provenance": "gold", "judge_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "protocol_hash": "cda8cfafe9da", "prof_total_s": 582.27, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 582.27, "prof_tok_in": 587, "prof_tok_out": 2674, "prof_llm_calls": 2}
