{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T15:09:08.096358", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "no_mem", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_04a04a9b-226c-43fd-b319-d5e89743676f", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: If we assume all articles published by Nature in 2020 (articles, only, not book reviews/columns, etc) relied on statistical significance to justify their findings and they on average came to a p-value of 0.04, how many papers would be incorrect as to their claims of statistical significance? Round the value up to the next integer.", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "Nature published roughly 1,743 research articles in 2020. Assuming a nominal false-positive rate equal to the average p-value of 0.04, the expected number of incorrect claims is 1,743 × 0.04 = 69.72. Rounding up to the next integer gives 70.\n\n70", "expected": "41", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 2, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": null, "fb_mode": "gold", "code_rev": "3f7502f7.ff40ae22", "critic_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "guarded", "score_provenance": "gold", "judge_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "protocol_hash": "cda8cfafe9da", "prof_total_s": 385.52, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 385.52, "prof_tok_in": 621, "prof_tok_out": 8401, "prof_llm_calls": 2}
