{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T21:03:36.398353", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "amem", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_708b99c5-e4a7-49cb-a5cf-933c8d46470d", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: On the DeepFruits fruit detection graph on Connected Papers from 2016, what feature caused the largest bubble to be the size it is?", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "Connected Papers visualizes academic literature as a network where each paper is represented by a circular node (bubble). According to the platform's documentation and standard design, the diameter of each bubble is scaled according to the total number of citations that specific paper has accumulated. The DeepFruits (2016) graph clusters it with neighboring works in deep learning and object detection, and the largest bubble on that graph belongs to the most highly cited paper in the cluster. Thus, the physical size of that largest bubble is driven entirely by how heavily the corresponding paper has been cited relative to the others.\n\nThe number of citations (citation count) of the paper.", "expected": "Citations", "score": 1.0, "em": 1.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 1.0, "fb_mode": "gold", "code_rev": "56816631.fcf27e88", "critic_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "guarded", "score_provenance": "gold", "judge_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "protocol_hash": "65cea4ee79fd", "prof_total_s": 579.45, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 579.45, "prof_tok_in": 537, "prof_tok_out": 1759, "prof_llm_calls": 2}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T21:03:37.472112", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "amem", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_0a3cd321-3e76-4622-911b-0fda2e5d6b1a", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: According to the World Bank, which countries had gross savings of over 35% of GDP for every year in the period 2001-2010? Give your answer as a comma-separated list of countries in alphabetical order. Use the countries most common names in english when answering.", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "```python\nimport urllib.request, json, time\n\nindicator = \"NY.GNS.ICTR.ZS\"\nurl = f\"https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/all/indicator/{indicator}?date=2001:2010&format=json&per_page=1000\"\nprint(url)\ntry:\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(url, timeout=30) as response:\n        data = json.loads(response.read().decode())\n    print(\"header:\", data[0])\n    print(\"records:\", len(data[1]) if data[1] else 0)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"Error:\", e)\n```\n\n```python\n# Let's inspect a few records\nif data and len(data)>1 and data[1]:\n    for rec in data[1][:5]:\n        print(rec)\n```\n\n```python\n# Need to paginate if total > per_page. Check header\nheader = data[0]\nprint(header)\n```\n\n```python\n# Fetch all pages\nimport math\nall_records = []\npage = 1\nper_page = 1000\nwhile True:\n    url = f\"https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/all/indicator/{indicator}?date=2001:2010&format=json&per_page={per_page}&page={page}\"\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(url, timeout=30) as r:\n        d = json.loads(r.read().decode())\n    if not d[1]:\n        break\n    all_records.extend(d[1])\n    total = d[0]['total']\n    pages = math.ceil(total/per_page)\n    if page >= pages:\n        break\n    page += 1\n    time.sleep(0.2)\nprint(\"fetched\", len(all_records))\n```\n\n```python\n# Organize by country\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nby_country = defaultdict(dict)\nfor rec in all_records:\n    country = rec['country']['value']\n    iso = rec['countryiso3code']\n    year = int(rec['date'])\n    val = rec['value']\n    if val is not None:\n        by_country[(country, iso)][year] = val\n\n# Check condition: years 2001-2010 all present and >35\nyears_needed = list(range(2001,2011))\nqualifying = []\nfor (country, iso), yvals in by_country.items():\n    if all(y in yvals and yvals[y] > 35 for y in years_needed):\n        qualifying.append((country, iso))\nprint(\"count:\", len(qualifying))\nfor q in sorted(qualifying):\n    print(q)\n```\n\n```python\n# Let's also print values for some candidates to verify\nfor (country, iso), yvals in by_country.items():\n    if all(y in yvals and yvals[y] > 35 for y in years_needed):\n        print(country, [round(yvals[y],1) for y in years_needed])\n```\n\n```python\n# Some countries may have aggregate names like World, Euro area, etc. We need countries only.\n# The API includes regions. We can filter by iso3 code if length==3? Actually countryiso3code for aggregates is empty or region code? Let's inspect.\n# Let's list iso for qualifying\nfor q in qualifying:\n    print(q)\n```\n\n```python\n# Let's get list of all country iso3 from World Bank countries API to distinguish economies.\nurl2 = \"https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country?format=json&per_page=1000\"\nwith urllib.request.urlopen(url2, timeout=30) as r:\n    cdata = json.loads(r.read().decode())\ncountries_meta = cdata[1]\n# build set of iso3 for actual countries (exclude aggregates). World Bank 'region' value? Let's inspect one aggregate.\nprint(countries_meta[0])\n```\n\n```python\n# Let's see which entries have iso3code length 3 and are not aggregates. The 'capitalCity' etc. But aggregates also have iso3? Let's check.\n# Print some with region 'Aggregates'\nagg = [c for c in countries_meta if c['region']['value'] == 'Aggregates']\nprint(\"aggregates count\", len(agg))\nprint([c['iso3Code'] for c in agg[:10]])\n```\n\n```python\n# So we can exclude any iso3 in aggregate set. But our qualifying list may include some aggregates like 'World' etc. Let's filter.\nagg_iso = {c['iso3Code'] for c in agg}\nqualifying_countries = [(c,i) for (c,i) in qualifying if i not in agg_iso and i != '']\nprint(\"filtered count:\", len(qualifying_countries))\nfor c,i in sorted(qualifying_countries):\n    print(c, i)\n```\n\n```python\n# Let's double-check if any missing data imputed? We required present. But maybe some countries have data for all years but not >35? We have list.\n# Let's also verify with World Bank website maybe. But first output names alphabetical by common English name.\nnames = sorted([c for (c,i) in qualifying_countries])\nprint(names)\n```\n\n```python\n# Let's inspect values for these to ensure >35 each year.\nfor (c,i) in qualifying_countries:\n    yv = by_country[(c,i)]\n    print(c, [round(yv[y],1) for y in years_needed])\n```\n\n```python\n# Wait, maybe there are countries with >35 every year but data missing for some year (e.g., late reporting). The question says \"according to the World Bank, which countries had gross savings of over 35% of GDP for every year in the period 2001-2010?\" If World Bank doesn't have data for a year, can we say they had? Probably not. So require data.\n# But maybe the dataset uses \"gross savings\" from World Bank's \"World Development Indicators\" and some countries missing. Let's see if any major candidates like China, Singapore, etc. Our list maybe includes: China, Singapore, etc? Let's see.\nprint(names)\n```\n\n```python\n# Let's also check if there are countries with code like 'HKG' etc. Need common English names. The API returns \"Hong Kong SAR, China\", \"Singapore\", etc. We'll use those as common names? The question says \"Use the countries most common names in english\". For Hong Kong maybe \"Hong Kong\"? But it's not a country, but a territory. The question says countries; but World Bank includes economies. We'll include as named by World Bank? Let's see list.\n# Let's print all qualifying including aggregates to see.\nfor q in qualifying:\n    print(q)\n```\n\n```python\n# Let's also verify indicator code is correct. Search web quickly? We'll do a web search to confirm \"Gross savings (% of GDP) World Bank NY.GNS.ICTR.ZS\". But we can trust API. However we should verify that the indicator is indeed gross savings. Let's search.\n```\n\n```python\n# Let's also consider that gross savings can be negative? But >35 positive.\n# Let's output final list after verifying with web search maybe.\n```\n\nNow we need to ensure our data is correct. Let's run the code and see output. I have written multiple code blocks but they are sequential? In this environment, each code block is executed separately? Actually the tool may execute each code block independently, not sharing variables. I need to be careful: The previous code blocks may not share state. In this chat, I think each code execution is separate? The system says code execution tool. Usually it's stateful within a session? Not sure. Better to write one comprehensive script that does everything and prints results. Let's do that. Also need to handle possible missing internet? Let's try a single script.</think:6124c78e>```python\nimport urllib.request, json, math, time\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nindicator = \"NY.GNS.ICTR.ZS\"\n# Fetch indicator data\nall_records = []\npage = 1\nper_page = 1000\nwhile True:\n    url = f\"https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/all/indicator/{indicator}?date=2001:2010&format=json&per_page={per_page}&page={page}\"\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(url, timeout=30) as r:\n        d = json.loads(r.read().decode())\n    if not d[1]:\n        break\n    all_records.extend(d[1])\n    total = d[0]['total']\n    pages = math.ceil(total/per_page)\n    if page >= pages:\n        break\n    page += 1\n    time.sleep(0.1)\n\n# Fetch country list to exclude aggregates\nurl2 = \"https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country?format=json&per_page=1000\"\nwith urllib.request.urlopen(url2, timeout=30) as r:\n    cdata = json.loads(r.read().decode())\ncountries_meta = cdata[1]\nagg_iso = {c['iso3Code'] for c in countries_meta if c['region']['value'] == 'Aggregates'}\n\nby_country = defaultdict(dict)\nfor rec in all_records:\n    country = rec['country']['value']\n    iso = rec['countryiso3code']\n    year = int(rec['date'])\n    val = rec['value']\n    if val is not None:\n        by_country[(country, iso)][year] = val\n\nyears_needed = list(range(2001,2011))\nqualifying = []\nfor (country, iso), yvals in by_country.items():\n    if iso in agg_iso or iso == '':\n        continue\n    if all(y in yvals and yvals[y] > 35 for y in years_needed):\n        qualifying.append((country, iso))\n\nqualifying.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\nprint(\"Number of qualifying economies:\", len(qualifying))\nfor c, i in qualifying:\n    yv = by_country[(c,i)]\n    print(c, i, [round(yv[y],1) for y in years_needed])\n\nnames = [c for c,i in qualifying]\nprint(\"\\nAlphabetical names:\")\nprint(names)\n```\n\n```python\n# Let's also verify by checking the World Bank indicator name from the API metadata.\nurl_meta = f\"https://api.worldbank.org/v2/indicator/{indicator}?format=json\"\nwith urllib.request.urlopen(url_meta, timeout=30) as r:\n    meta = json.loads(r.read().decode())\nprint(meta[1][0]['name'])\n```\n\nThe previous code blocks may not have executed because I didn't actually run them? Wait, the system shows my messages with code blocks but no tool results. In this interface, I need to actually call the tools? The user message says \"Think step by step. Use tools as needed.\" I have access to web search, file reading, code execution, and computation tools. But in this conversation, I haven't seen tool results. Perhaps I need to invoke the tools via specific syntax? The instructions say I can use tools. In this environment, maybe I should output a function call? But the system didn't provide a tool calling format. Usually in these tasks, the assistant writes code blocks and the platform executes them