{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T21:04:33.164249", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "amem", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_2b3ef98c-cc05-450b-a719-711aee40ac65", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: Could you help me out with this assignment? Our professor sprung it on us at the end of class Friday, and I'm still trying to figure it out. The question he asked us was about an anagram. I've attached an audio recording of the question that he asked, so if you could please take a listen and give me the answer, I'd really appreciate the help. Please limit your response to the anagram text that could be generated from the original line which fu", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "I cannot access any audio attachment in this text-only environment, and no tools are available to play or transcribe audio. Therefore, I cannot determine the original line spoken by the professor or the specific anagram request. Without that source text, I am unable to generate the requested anagram.\n\n", "expected": "To be or not to be that is the question whether tis nobler in the mind to suffer the slings and arrows of outrageous fortune", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 0.0, "fb_mode": "gold", "code_rev": "56816631.fcf27e88", "critic_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "guarded", "score_provenance": "gold", "judge_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "protocol_hash": "65cea4ee79fd", "prof_total_s": 602.13, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 602.13, "prof_tok_in": 719, "prof_tok_out": 3856, "prof_llm_calls": 2}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T21:04:36.734614", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "amem", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_04a04a9b-226c-43fd-b319-d5e89743676f", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: If we assume all articles published by Nature in 2020 (articles, only, not book reviews/columns, etc) relied on statistical significance to justify their findings and they on average came to a p-value of 0.04, how many papers would be incorrect as to their claims of statistical significance? Round the value up to the next integer.", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "Nature published roughly 1,139 research articles in 2020. Assuming an average p‑value of 0.04 implies an expected false‑positive fraction of about 4% (0.04). Thus 1,139 × 0.04 = 45.56, which rounds up to the next integer:\n\n46", "expected": "41", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 0.0, "fb_mode": "gold", "code_rev": "56816631.fcf27e88", "critic_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "guarded", "score_provenance": "gold", "judge_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "protocol_hash": "65cea4ee79fd", "prof_total_s": 592.22, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 592.22, "prof_tok_in": 621, "prof_tok_out": 11901, "prof_llm_calls": 2}
