{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T21:23:05.106100", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "amem", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_708b99c5-e4a7-49cb-a5cf-933c8d46470d", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: On the DeepFruits fruit detection graph on Connected Papers from 2016, what feature caused the largest bubble to be the size it is?", "augmented_prompt": "## Memories from earlier attempts (A-Mem)\n\n- Task: Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: On the DeepFruits fruit detection graph on Connected Papers from 2016, what feature caused the largest bubble to be the size it is?\nAttempt: Connected Papers sizes each node (bubble) by the paper’s citation count, so the largest bubble is the one with the most citations.\n\nFinal answer: The number of citations the paper has received (its ci\n- Task: Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: On the DeepFruits fruit detection graph on Connected Papers from 2016, what feature caused the largest bubble to be the size it is?\nAttempt: Connected Papers visualizes academic literature as a network where each paper is represented by a circular node (bubble). According to the platform's documentation and standard design, the diameter of", "response": "Connected Papers visualizes academic papers as a network of bubbles where each node represents a paper. According to the platform's documentation, the diameter (size) of each bubble is proportional to the number of citations that paper has received. Therefore, on the DeepFruits 2016 graph, the largest bubble corresponds to the paper with the highest citation count among those displayed, and its size is determined by that citation count.\n\nThe number of citations the paper has received (its citation count)", "expected": "Citations", "score": 1.0, "em": 1.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": true, "aug_len": 848, "iteration": 2, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": f